VulkanNetwork — UDP, reliable datagrams and authenticated encryption.

Public headers:
  network/network.hpp
  network/endpoint.hpp         IPv4/IPv6 address and peer identity
  network/socket.hpp           low-level unencrypted UDP; internal transport use
  network/secure.hpp           certificate-verified, mutually authenticated DTLS 1.2
  network/stream.hpp           reliable messages, ordering-independent, congestion window
  network/session.hpp          reliable channels over a supplied datagram sender
  network/host.hpp             low-level dedicated UDP host (unencrypted)

Secure transport:
- net::secure::create(role, settings, local_port, bind_host) initializes Mbed TLS.
- Default automatic mode is enabled explicitly with settings.automatic_identity=true,
  storage_directory=<persistent user config path>. Client sets trust_identity to
  the stable configured host:port to pin the destination separately per endpoint.
- On first startup the server generates a P-256 private key at storage_directory/
  server.key using the Mbed TLS RNG; it remains on disk with restricted access.
  Every server launch generates a fresh one-year self-signed certificate using
  the persisted key. Rotating the certificate does not change its public-key pin.
- Client does NOT need a key, certificate, CA or external OpenSSL executable.
  It verifies the remote certificate's public key against a stored SHA-256
  SubjectPublicKeyInfo fingerprint; on first contact the key is pinned under
  storage_directory/pins/<hash-of-configured-host:port>.pin.
- WARNING: first contact is trust-on-first-use. The client cannot know it
  connected to the intended server if an attacker intercepted the very first
  handshake. For high-assurance external servers, use an independent trust
  anchor or verify the server's displayed key fingerprint out-of-band.
- A changed public key after initial enrollment is rejected. Never
  silently regenerate client pins after unexpected identity changes.
- Optional explicit strict mTLS remains supported by supplying ca_file,
  certificate_file, private_key_file, and server_name (client); this uses
  VERIFY_REQUIRED on both ends with trusted X.509 chains.
- Both modes encrypt and authenticate all application data through DTLS,
  with no plaintext game traffic or fallback.
- All peers on a UDP socket get distinct Mbed TLS contexts, and the DTLS
  record layer gives confidentiality, integrity and replay protection.
- The library's peer map is single-thread owned; do not concurrently call
  send/receive/destroy on one resource without synchronization.
- net::session uses net::secure::send as its datagram sender and sends
  reliable and fragmentable messages through the secured tunnel.

Protocol restrictions:
- Both endpoints must run the same version of the application and wire
  protocol. A previous plaintext VSN1/game client cannot join a secure server.
- DTLS is not a complete authorization system: the game still enforces
  game-session tokens and endpoint ownership after certificate verification.
- In automatic mode the server does NOT authenticate client identities.
  Application-level player admission, permissions and bans are still needed.
  Certificate pinning alone does not authorize a player.
- Explicit mTLS mode authenticates certificates, not game accounts.
- Bounded queues and DTLS cookies reduce trivial resource attacks but do not
  constitute DDoS protection. Nonblocking handshake and packet-loss testing
  are essential before deploying on an untrusted Internet network.
- DTLS 1.2 provides no QUIC-style stream transport, congestion-managed
  connection multiplexing, certificate provisioning or NAT traversal.
- Low-level net::socket and net::host are intentionally unencrypted. Do not
  expose those APIs to external/untrusted game traffic.

Build:
  The c build tool exports Git files without submodules. network/mbedtls_prepare.cmake
  is injected before the upstream Mbed TLS project configuration, fetches the
  Mbed TLS framework dependency at the exact upstream Gitlink revision
  dde0c4a0e448a0552f18817dcea633bb851fd288, and verifies its commit.
  This dependency needs Git access during first-time CMake configuration.
  In offline environments, pre-populate the pinned framework in the Mbed TLS
  source cache. Unavailable or mismatched framework sources fail the build.

  Mbed TLS 3.6.7 source is pinned and built as a static CMake dependency
  of VulkanNetwork (mbedtls, mbedx509, mbedcrypto).
  Mbed TLS is Apache-2.0 licensed.

